// The section "Components" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Components() {
    return <>
        <p>
            A component is declared with <code>comp</code> and used as a tag with a capital letter.
            Its parameters are its properties: they are checked like the arguments of a function,
            they can have default values, and <code>children Content</code> gets the markup between
            the tags. An object with the properties can be passed whole:
            {" "}<code>{"<Product {...product} />"}</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Components do not exist in JS: every use is replaced by the component's code. The
            {" "}<b>JavaScript</b> tab shows only the elements being created. When the copies of
            a component would add more than 100 lines to a function and its calls, the component
            becomes a function instead. Elements are created by <code>$$element</code> of the
            {" "}runtime, which takes their attributes.
        </p>
        <Example name="components" />
        <p>
            The last property <code>...attrs</code> takes the attributes of a use that are not
            properties, and <code>{"{...attrs}"}</code> puts them on one element of the component.
            They are checked as attributes of that element, so a button keeps all of its own:
            {" "}<code>disabled</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>aria-*</code>, handlers.
        </p>
        <Example name="attrs" />
        <p>
            Properties are read-only, except bound ones. A component that changes a value of the
            parent, a panel that closes or tabs that switch, declares the property with
            {" "}<code>bind</code>, <code>{"comp Panel(bind open bool)"}</code>, and changes it as
            its state. The use writes the changes into its variable with
            {" "}<code>{"bind:open={menu}"}</code>, as <code>bind:value</code> of an input does,
            so it needs no pair of <code>open</code> and <code>onClose</code>. Without
            {" "}<code>bind:</code> the property is the component's own state. The item of a
            {" "}<code>for</code> can be bound too, <code>{"bind:todo={todo}"}</code>: what the
            component changes in it updates the list.
        </p>
        <Example name="component-bind" />
        <p>
            A component can use itself, for a tree or nested comments. Such a component cannot be
            inlined, so it becomes a function, and every call of it has its own state. The recursion
            must end: the compiler accepts a use inside <code>{"{if ...}"}</code> or
            {" "}<code>{"{for ...}"}</code> or after an early <code>return</code>, but not an
            unconditional one. An early <code>return</code> picks the markup once, when the
            component is created.
        </p>
        <Example name="tree" />
        <p>
            A component exported with <code>export comp</code> is imported by its name, as in
            {" "}<code>{'import { Card } from "./card.rk"'}</code>, and used as a tag. Its module
            compiles it to a function, which other modules call, or inline it when it is small,
            by the same 100 lines.
        </p>
        <p>
            A component is also a value: it can go into an array, a property or
            {" "}<code>state</code>. Its type lists the properties with their names,
            {" "}<code>comp(name string)</code>, and a tag takes the component from a value: a tag
            with a dot, <code>{"<tab.content />"}</code>, or a variable with a capital letter,
            {" "}<code>{"<Current />"}</code>. The tag passes the properties in the order of the
            type, so a component fits it with the same names in the same order. Such a component
            becomes a function; when the value changes, it is created again.
        </p>
        <Example name="component-values" />
    </>
}
